Romi16 Free-to-Play License
Version 1.1 — 5 September 2026
Attribution corrected 21 September 2026; free-play terms retained.

Michael K's original contributions to the 2001 Romi16 game:
Copyright (c) 2001 Michael K. All rights reserved except as granted below.

Michael K designed and programmed the original game, including the world model,
lava, skydome and map textures, texts, writing/script, and main source code
(excluding third-party libraries). Switch-texture authorship is probable but
unconfirmed. Most remaining assets, including tiling textures, sounds and music,
came from third-party resources. Their copyright belongs to their respective
holders. The lamp-on image was modified from a third-party lamp-off image.

Romi K guided the modern conversion, verification, and publication. OpenAI Codex
generated the new WebGL code through AI translation and adaptation of the
original implementation. These are contribution credits, not a transfer of
rights or a copyright claim by the AI. See CREDITS.txt and ASSET_PROVENANCE.txt.

1. Free play

The copyright holders grant everyone a worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free
permission to download, store, copy for their own use, and play the released
compiled Romi16 game, without payment. This includes playing in a browser and
serving an unmodified copy on a local server for personal or household play.
This permission continues for copies lawfully obtained under these terms,
provided these terms are followed. Keep the included credits and notices intact.

2. Rights reserved

This license grants no right to sell, sublicense, publicly redistribute or host,
modify, or reuse the game's code or assets in other works. Those uses require
separate permission from the relevant copyright holders. No source-code access
or source-code distribution is promised or required by this license. All rights
not expressly granted are reserved. Nothing here limits rights available under
applicable law or rights independently granted under other licenses.

3. Third-party components

Third-party components remain governed by their own licenses. In particular,
three.js is licensed under MIT and webaudio-tinysynth under Apache-2.0. Their
notices and license texts are included in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt. The limits
above do not restrict permissions those licenses independently grant.

This also distinguishes the legacy third-party textures, sounds, music and
lamp imagery from Michael K's original work. Their exact sources and permissions
remain unresolved in the current records; modern library notices do not cover
them. This document speaks only for rights its contributors are authorized to
grant and does not establish permission from unidentified third-party holders.
Optional generated derivatives do not resolve the rights of their references.
See ASSET_PROVENANCE.txt for the evidence and the limits of this correction.

